'''Guajicutea''', officially the '''Republic of Guajicutea''', is an island country in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]] on the planet [[Adonia]]. It is the largest island in the Greater Capuyaquiran archipelago and has an area of approximately 3,278 square kilometres. The country had an estimated population of 2,842,119 in 2025. Its capital and largest city is [[Waribá]]. Other major cities include [[Waku Bá]], [[Barikú]], [[Tabakurí]], and [[Yukubá]].

Guajicutea is governed as a unitary presidential republic. The country has 17 districts, including the separate island district of [[Warikú]], and approximately 59 municipalities. The [[Asamblea Nacional]] is elected through a mixed-member proportional system. Guaji and Sartan are the principal languages of public life; Volisanian is widely used in tourism, hospitality, and regional commerce.

The island's population is predominantly [[Guaji people|Guaji]], an Arawak-speaking people whose society developed around volcanic agriculture, coastal navigation, and inter-island trade. Guajicutean history also includes substantial Bayani, Kaftian, Sartan, and Zong communities formed through migration, colonial rule, slavery, refugee movements, and modern regional mobility. The country has a religiously diverse population, with Petran Catholicism as the largest tradition, alongside Sunni Islam, the [[Three Powers faith]], the [[Three Powers Revival]], Burawi Catholicism, Protestantism, Catharism, and Tianism.

== Etymology ==

The name ''Guajicutea'' derives from the name of the Guaji people and the island on which their society developed. The name has been used for the island, its pre-colonial political communities, the colonial administrations that ruled it, and the modern republic. The modern demonym is '''Guajicutean'''; the indigenous ethnonym is '''Guaji'''.

== Geography ==

Guajicutea is a volcanic island located between the Greater and Lesser Capuyaquiran archipelagos. Its northern highlands contain the peaks of [[Cerro Guanarí]], [[Cerro Caracurí]], [[Cerro Trogoní]], [[Cerro Vienturí]], and [[Monte Yukumá]], the island's principal volcano. The highest elevations and steepest terrain are concentrated in the north and centre, while the southern and western lowlands contain the largest areas of intensive agriculture and plantation settlement.

The island's main ports are [[Yukubá]] on the south-western coast, [[Waribá]] on the north-eastern coast, and [[Barikú]] on the western coast. Waribá faces the Greater Capuyaquiran islands and became the principal modern port and industrial centre. Yukubá historically connected Guajicutea with the Lesser Capuyaquiran and mainland [[Tapuya]], while Barikú served as a redistribution port on the southern route around Monte Yukumá.

The modern districts reflect the former Guaji bikabás, although their boundaries and administrative functions have changed over time. Ganan, Taban, and Venturan form the principal population spine. The interior and highland districts, including Yukuman, Warikú, Barikan, Bukan, Wakunan, Kurun, and Makuan, are more sparsely settled.

