== History ==

{{main|History of Guajicutea}}

=== Pre-colonial period ===

Guaji settlement on the island dates to at least the first centuries of the Common Era. The earliest documented arrival tradition is preserved by the [[Tabayabí stone]], a sixth-century chronicle that records events reaching back to approximately 215–220 CE, although the interpretation of its arrival imagery remains disputed. The early Guaji population included the western highland [[Yukurí]] and eastern and northern maritime [[Caracurí]] traditions. Their societies traded, intermarried, and fought before being politically and culturally unified after the [[Yukubara]] volcanic mudflow of 516 CE.

The year 516 became the first year of the Guaji lunar calendar. After the disaster, the Guaji developed a unified political and religious system, expanded agriculture and maritime trade, and built the catacomb complex known as the [[Catacumbas del Abismo]]. Guaji society was organized through villages, chiefly lineages, religious specialists, merchants, farmers, fishermen, craftsmen, and warrior groups. Authority was divided between the elected [[Cacique Má]] and the [[Bohíque Má]], or Warí, the highest priestess.

From approximately 1290 to 1360, Guajicutea established a tributary network across eleven islands of the Greater Capuyaquiran. It was a maritime power rather than a territorial empire. Tribute was collected by canoe fleets and included cotton, shells, pearls, food, wood, and marriage partners. The [[Kari Wirá]] war against the Tapuyic Empire lasted from 1344 to 1392 and ended with the Treaty of Kariwári. A period of peace and trade followed. Copper mining, large sewn-plank vessels, maize cultivation, and bronze working expanded between the late fourteenth and early sixteenth centuries.

The northern voyage of [[Kari Buka]] from 1502 to 1514, later recorded in the [[Kari Yabí]] chronicle, brought Guaji navigators to the coast of the [[Aruanãyára]] Empire. The voyage demonstrated the reach of Guaji navigation but did not produce a lasting direct tin trade before colonial intervention.

=== Colonial transformation ===

Burawi explorer [[Guled Badweyn]] made brief contact with Guajicutea on 5 December 1520. Burawi trade and missionary activity expanded after 1523, followed by Volisanian and Sartan involvement. Smallpox caused the [[Great Pestilence]] from 1531, killing approximately 30 percent of the population and contributing to famine and political instability.

The [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]] was established in 1536 as a Burawi vassal state. Its rulers used the Burawi title [[Boqor]] rather than Cacique Má. The kingdom was affected by religious conflict, the [[First Guaji Civil War]] of 1554, Volisanian expansion, plantation agriculture, and the introduction of chattel slavery in 1543. Slavery ended in 1571 after economic depression and a Guaji peasant rebellion; many formerly enslaved Kaftians were absorbed into the population.

Sarta conquered Guajicutea during the [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] of 1608–1609 and established the [[Sartan Guajicutea|Sarta Colony of Guajicutea]]. Colonial rule brought centralized administration, a strict Sartan language policy, migration from Bayani communities, and major naval and industrial works at Waribá. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] and [[Castillo de San Esteban]] made Waribá the principal military and manufacturing centre of the Capuyaquiran Sea. The [[Sarta-Volisania War]] of 1683–1705 caused economic collapse, famine, unrest, and the temporary overthrow of the colonial government in 1691.

=== Republic and occupation ===

Sarta transferred control to the [[Padres de Guajicutea]] in 1723 after the [[Peaceful Revolution of 1723]], establishing the First Republic. The early republic was dominated by Sartan and mixed landowners, but parish education and industrialization gradually expanded political participation. Universal citizenship was established in 1803, and women received the right to vote in 1904.

The [[Northern Confederation]] invaded Guajicutea in 1812 and imposed a military occupation. Racial classification laws, restrictions on marriage, censorship, and political repression were followed by the [[Bloody Decade]] of 1814–1822. The [[Guarda Revolutionary]], led by [[Tomás Yukurí]] and [[Diego Nasarí]], resisted the occupation. The [[Virtuous Revolution]] of 1824 restored the republican constitution.

Industrialization, immigration, and urbanization accelerated in the second half of the nineteenth century. The [[War of Liberation]] against Lacashe ended slavery in the region in 1856 and produced a major Kaftian refugee movement to Guajicutea. The [[Movimiento de la Warí]] secured women's suffrage, while the rediscovery of the catacombs in 1892 contributed to the [[Bikuismo movement]].

=== Fascism and the Great Adonian War ===

Fascist influence from Volisania and Sarta grew after 1917. Emergency rule began in 1930 under [[Damián Cordero]], and opposition parties were banned. Guajicutea industrialized rapidly as a military-manufacturing base. During the [[Great Adonian War]], Zong residents were interned at [[Campo de Las Minas]], the island was subjected to bombing, and the government relinquished control to Volisania in February 1943. The [[Grupo de Liberación Guajicuteano]] conducted an armed resistance campaign, including the [[Battles of the Caves]]. Volisanian forces withdrew after the liberal revolution of 3 July 1947.

The Third Republic was established on 18 December 1947. Its constitution guaranteed full enfranchisement, freedom of speech and assembly, religious and language protections, citizenship regardless of race, restrictions on foreign ownership, and universal education. Guajicutea joined the [[Adonian Community]] in 1948.

=== Contemporary period ===

Post-war reconstruction transformed Waribá into a centre of tourism, hospitality, and specialty agriculture. The state-run Programa de Hospitalidad, national airline, and cruise network supported rapid population growth and immigration during the 1950s and 1960s. Economic instability followed the Camboriú War and oil shock of the late 1970s. The government of [[Julián Mera]] stabilized the country through private resort development, foreign investment, and restrictions on political opposition, while also creating greater regional inequality.

The [[May Revolution]] of 2000 ended the Mera political system after the death of Julián Mera, the suspected killing of former security chief [[Raúl Makuán]], mass protests, and international pressure. The subsequent government investigated secret prisons, disappearances, and mass graves associated with the former security services. Since 2012, Guajicutea has experienced growth in tourism, call-centre services, and offshore business processing, alongside recurrent labour disputes and housing pressures caused by foreign investment.

The COVID-19 pandemic produced a severe recession and political realignment. A coalition led by the [[Partido Kadarismo]], [[Partido Verde]], and [[Partido Popular Guaji]] won the 2022 election. [[Sofía Yayaí]] became president in 2024 through a coalition of the Partido Popular Guaji and [[Partido Bukaí]]. Contemporary political debates include housing affordability, pensions, immigration, environmental protection, tourism dependence, and the place of Guaji language and culture in public life.

== Politics ==

Guajicutea is a unitary presidential republic. The president is directly elected for a four-year term, and the [[Asamblea Nacional]] is elected through mixed-member proportional representation. Legislative representation combines single-member district seats with party-list seats. The 17 districts allocate 284 assembly seats in total, approximately one seat per 10,000 residents.

District governors are directly elected. Municipalities are governed by six-member councils, with half of each council elected every two years. Three designated ciudades — Waribá, Barikú, and Tabakurí — coordinate services across municipal boundaries through city executive councils. Barrios are the smallest administrative units and are managed through local committees and associations.

The principal active political parties are the [[Partido Laborista de Guajicutea]], Partido Popular Guaji, Partido Kadarismo, Partido Bukaí, Partido Yayaí, and Partido Verde. Earlier parties, including the Partido Unidad y Prosperidad and Partido Restauración, remain important in the political history of the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries.

== Economy ==

Guajicutea has a service-oriented economy based on tourism, hospitality, call centres, offshore business processing, transport, food processing, and light manufacturing. Tourism remains an important source of employment and foreign exchange, although the economy is less dependent on mass tourism than during the state hospitality era of the mid-twentieth century. Resort development is concentrated along the coast and around airport and port corridors.

Agriculture includes sugar, cassava, yams, Makú, maize, tobacco, dyes, indigo, annatto, specialty crops, and dried fish production. Bariña, a sugar-cane spirit developed during the colonial period, is a national heritage product. Wariguma, a maize spirit associated with the northern highlands, has gained domestic recognition but remains less prominent internationally.

Guajicutea uses the Guajicutean sol, written GꞨ. The currency is managed against the Sartan peso. Modern economic challenges include housing costs, foreign ownership of property, dependence on imported goods, unequal access to coastal development, and the environmental effects of intensive tourism and urbanization.

== Demographics ==

The population was estimated at 2,842,119 in 2025. Waribá is the largest metropolitan area, followed by Waku Bá, Barikú, Tabakurí, and Yukubá. Approximately half the population is concentrated in the Ganan, Taban, and Venturan districts, while the northern and interior districts have lower population densities.

Guaji people form the largest ethnic group. Bayani communities are concentrated in urban and port districts and include Sunni Muslim and Cathar households. Kaftian Guajicutean communities descend both from colonial-era enslaved people and later migration and refugee movements. Sartan and mixed communities are concentrated in cities, administration, commerce, and professional services. Zong Guajicutean communities are a small but historically significant population whose presence is connected to trade, wartime internment, and post-war migration.

Guaji and Sartan are the principal languages. Guaji remains widely used in rural communities, family life, cultural institutions, and the Warí Revival. Sartan is dominant in much formal administration and commerce. Volisanian is widely used in tourism and hospitality, particularly for visitors from Balboa and Tapuya. Baliscano is present in regional commercial and migrant networks.

== Culture ==

Guajicutean culture combines Guaji maritime and agricultural traditions with Sartan, Bayani, Kaftian, Zong, Burawi, and Volisanian influences. Guaji cultural revival movements have promoted Bikumaku writing, traditional symbolism, the history of the Bohíques, and the authority associated with the Warí. The [[Bikumaku Language Act of 2021]] gave Bikumaku official recognition and support in public life.

Traditional Guaji religion recognizes three living powers associated with the sea, mountain, and sky. The modern [[Three Powers faith]] preserves older practices, while the [[Three Powers Revival]] is a growing urban cultural and religious movement. Petran Catholicism is the largest religious tradition, and Burawi Catholicism remains concentrated in families and neighbourhoods with older Burawi ties. Sunni Islam is the principal religion of the Bayani population.

Guajicutean foodways include cassava flour, yams, Makú, maize, dried fish, tobacco, cane products, and imported grains and textiles. Bariña is commonly served neat, chilled, or over ice. Contemporary arts draw on Bikuismo, realistic depictions of historical violence, Guaji symbolic forms, and the island's maritime landscape.

